      "Build in a predictable \"reset\" spot: a specific corner, cushion, or quiet space your child can go to when things feel like too much, that isn't associated with being in trouble. The value isn't the physical space itself, it's the predictability of having somewhere to go before things escalate, rather than only reacting once they have.",
      "Adjust the environment before adjusting the child. If bath time is consistently hard, it's often worth changing the water temperature, the lighting, or the specific soap before assuming the child needs to \"get used to it.\" Sensory sensitivities are usually real physiological responses, not preferences to be trained away.",
      "None of this replaces an occupational therapist who specializes in sensory processing, who can identify your child's specific sensory profile far more precisely than home observation alone. But a calmer home routine makes whatever they're working on in session easier to reinforce the other six days of the week.",
